oping iron.i NBS  for regenerative medicine and cell  therapy but again as I've mentioned a  few times now we have some pretty  obvious limitations and challenges  before us we have a very limited amount  of space that we have to work with and a  very limited staff that we have  to perform all of the development  activities and manufacturing activities  so I want to give you an idea of what  our manual manufacturing protocols were  looking like we use the cell processing  workstation as you saw in the images  earlier if you're not familiar the cell  processing workstation is a standalone  isolator type of system it is a class  100 environment on the inside once it's  been decontaminated it has its own  incorporated hydrogen peroxide  decontamination system it also has  everything else that you need to do cell  culture like a centrifuge and microscope  and an incubator the incubators are  modular so you can add additional  incubators if you need to but we don't  have the space for that so we just have  one modular incubator that's only 1.4  cubic feet on the inside contrast that  with a standard cell culture incubator  that has about 6 cubic feet of space on  the inside so we are working with a much  less space than what we're used to  having to handle all of our incubation  means we use multi layer flasks and with  all the options out there on the market  we are able to narrow it down to two  different types that we could use with  this modular incubator that those were  the millah pour high flasks which have  about a thousand square centimeters of  growth area and the corning hyper flask  which are tea 1720s this is what they  look like and for all of our  manufacturing methods we use alpha mem  and 5% platelet lysate we've gotten away  from SPS some time ago and this is how  we would do it we would start by  expanding the MSBs up to passage 1 to  create a master cell bank of vials of  MSC to do that we were able to scale up  to a maximum with 10 p1 thousands last  that's the maximum number of flat so we  can fit inside the modular incubator in  those 10 to 1,000 flasks we could  produce enough cells about 200 million  MSPs to freeze down about 100 vials of 2  million MFCs per vial for our masters  Elding  after that when we're ready to create a  batch of product we could pull vials  from the master cell bank and further  expand them up to passage 3 to create  batches of stem cell products that are  intended to be used for therapy to do  that we could scale up to a maximum of  10 t 17/20 flasks and at the end of all  this we could approximately create about  4 doses of product and each each dose  would have a hundred million MFCs in the  bag from start to finish which is from  the raw bone marrow aspirate all the way  to creating the very first batch of stem  cell product doses that manufacturing  campaign would take anywhere from 6 to 8  weeks to complete so there are some  pretty obvious limitations to doing a  display first of all you can only fit a  maximum of 10 of either type of  multi-layer flasks inside the incubator  so you're really limited in how many  cells you can produce the whole process  from start to finish is extremely  tedious very time-consuming and  labor-intensive you really absolutely  need at least two technologists to  perform these tasks efficiently I have  done it with one before during the  development phase and it just is not  feasible you consume a huge number or a  huge volume of culture medium doing it  this way each of the Corning hyper  flasks requires five hundred and  sixty-five milliliters of culture medium  to fill up the flask completely which  you have to do with those flasks and so  you can imagine towards the end when you  have 10 hyper flasks and every time you  do a medium change you need 6 liters  approximately 6 liters of media so you  can see how the costs and of medium and  player life say it can really add up  doing it this way and it's a relatively  long period of time from start to finish  and at the end you really do not get  that many doses of products so you're  putting in a lot and you're not really  getting a lot out of it so it didn't  take long for us to realize that we  needed to make some changes and do some  things  differently so we started thinking about  bioreactors we looked at a few different  systems there were three different  systems that we considered that were  small footprint single-use platforms one  of them of course is the to remove ECT  quantum cell expansion system one of the  platforms required the use of micro  carriers and the other two platforms the  cells grew out in a more traditional two  dimensional model layer type of manner  however one of those platforms we had to  exclude kind of early on for space  considerations because although it was  indeed a small footprint type of  platform it still required a little bit  more instrumentation to go along with  the whole thing than we had space for so  in the end we narrowed it down to two  systems the micro carrier system in the  quantum and we brought them both  in-house and tested them for expanding  bone marrow MSPs so we thoroughly  evaluated these two systems and  eventually we decided that we had to  rule out the micro carrier system a big  part of this was the amount of  involvement in the time that we were  going to need to translate our current  2-d system to a 3d system because really  if you've never worked at micro carriers  before they're just so different from  growing cells and flasks and there's a  lot of steps that need to be worked out  and developed along the way and we did a  lot of this during the evaluation phase  we did micro carrier screening assays  where we looked at a whole bunch of  different micro carriers and tried to  figure out which ones were going to work  best for our needs and for our cell type  there was also considerations regarding  the way that you have to prepare the  micro carrier some of them are  pre-sterilized some of them need to be  autoclave some of them many of them need  to be pre hydrated and all these things  you have to think about and in fact we  don't have easy access to an autoclave  we have to walk across the street to get  one and so obviously this wasn't really  an ideal situation for us regarding  monitoring of the culture you have to  think about that not knowing when the  cells are going to be confluent on the  barriers and also you need to keep a  certain cell to micro carrier ratio so  after a certain amount of time you have  to add more micro carriers and you have  to figure out how to how to know when to  do that so again just more steps that  need more development and then there's  the question of how to harvest the cells  from the micro carriers and once you do  how will you separate the micro carriers  from the cells you may need additional  equipment like a fluidized bed  centrifuge a lot of the techniques micro  carry separation techniques that were on  the market at the time when we did this  evaluation were designed for very large  scale applications and not really for  processing volumes in the Frye's liter  range which is where we were and then  after all this how can you do your  purity testing how can you be sure that  you have no micro carrier contamination  in the final product so again these are  all things that we could have worked out  over time but each one of these steps  needed to be developed and tested and it  would have taken a very considerable  amount of time for the two technologists  that we had doing this work so again we  eventually had to rule this this  platform out so we narrowed our options  down to the platform but we still really  weren't sure if this was going to be  right for us there were some obvious  benefits to using the quantum like the  small footprint with the eliminated  space that we have and also the relative  ease of translating our current  protocols to using the bioreactor there  was a reduction in the amount of time it  took for us to expand the cells and also  we were able to after doing a number of  pilot runs with two remotes help get to  where we could produce greater cell  yields in the bioreactor versus using  flasks and we're also able to keep the  cells at a lower passage for banking and  for production of MSC products but  probably the most valuable advantage for  our particular situation was the  addition of automation because it saved  us so much time with performing manual  tasks  there's also the possibility of  scalability with a quantum we have a  5,000 square foot GMP facility that is  being planned out right now hopefully  for this year or next year and so  hopefully when we get this new facility  and we have a little bit more space we  can add more Quantum's and once we're  familiar with the protocol we can just  do the same protocol with more Quantum's  and able and we'll be able to get more  cell yield so I'm trying to determine  whether or not this platform was going  to be right for us we had to ask the  question whether or not this automated  platform was going to measure up and  every sense to the current manual  protocol that we were using we looked at  a number of different parameters and we  did the analysis and we were able to put  out this poster that we presented the  world stem cell somewhat a couple years  ago if any was interested in looking at  this poster please feel free to email me  but I'm going to go over what we found  in the subsequent slides and again to  highlight what what our overarching  goals were first and foremost we needed  the ability to expand large quantities  of MSC s so that we could treat patients  and cell therapy and more general  medicine clinical trials and manufacture  those cells in compliance with GMP  standards and so with this analysis we  wanted to be able to compare the  automated versus manual method with  regard to cell yields cost time and  effort from the technologist and just  overall ease of performing the protocols  so what we did was we started with 25 25  to 30 milliliters of fresh bone marrow  aspirate and we performed the automated  method and the manual method basically  side by side we isolate and expanded  Romero MSC s using alpha mem and 5%  human play-doh lysate we created master  cell banks using the automated method  and a manual method and products using  both methods as well and then we just  quantified the difference as we looked  at again so yields we looked at the cost  of consumables and reagents needed to  perform these two different methods how  long it took to grow the cells how much  effort it required from the  technologists and how many technologists  we needed to do all the work  first looking at the automated method  you're able to dilute the bone marrow  and add it directly to the quantum  system you don't have to pre isolate the  mono nuclear cells as you normally do  you do have to pre coat the bioreactor  with fiber net and overnight or else the  cells will not adhere in order to  monitor the culture you look at glucose  consumption lactate generation and you  can also see control flasks at the same  seating densities just to give you a  visual of how complement the cells are  to help aid in your decision as to when  the cells are ready to be harvested you  can freeze down cells in two vials as  early as p0 but you get a lot more cells  at p1 and so we typically expand up to  p1 to create our master cell bank but  you will have additional vials at p0  that you can further expand to p1 to  create more cells to Bank and then of  course eventually up to create more  products the product that product bags  can be generated as early as passage 2  so you pull vials from passage 1 from  the master cell back and then just  expand them up one more passage to  create doses and the total area service  area for growth inside one bioreactor is  21,000 square centimeters for the manual  method we first isolated the modern  nuclear cells from the bone marrow using  cycle and then feed them directly to  t1000 flasks that were not coated at  actually 50 to 100 thousand cells per  centimeter squared we provide a fresh  media every 3 to 4 days passage to the  cells when they were about 85 to 90%  confluent and we froze down master cell  bank files at passage 1 we then pulled  vials from the master cell bank to  expand products but we couldn't do that  we couldn't actually freeze products  down until passage 3 because you don't  have that many cells in your vial for  passage 1 so you have to first expand  them to passage 2 and then you can  really scale up by passage 3 so a very  important thing to understand about how  we did this analysis because we didn't  do this based on using the cell  processing workstation knowing most  people don't have one most people are  going to be doing this  these manufacturing processes in a  cleanroom with standard cell closable  camps standard cell culture incubators  so we wanted to sort of normalize this  so that knowing that everybody has a  different kind of situation and not  everybody has the same number of  incubators in their laboratories we  wanted to just kind of normalize the  whole analysis down to the number of  cells you could produce using one  standard size six cubic foot cell  culture incubator and so in one standard  six cubic foot cell culture incubator we  were able to fit a maximum of 48 corning  hyper flasks p1 1720 into one of those  incubators for maximum capacity is about  82,000 square centimeters for cell  growth and this is what we came up with  I know this is a busy slide and I will  summarize it later but so you can see  how we did it we looked first at all the  reagents and consumables that we would  need to perform each process using each  method and for all the passages that we  need to perform using each method these  are the cost for the quantum and the  required passages associated with using  the quantum and also the cost for  expanding manually and these are all of  the parameters that we looked at we  looked at how much did it cost to run  each passage that we would need to run  exactly how much time it would take to  actually expand each passage how much  hands-on time it would take from the  technologists actually performing tasks  and how many technologists you would  need to perform the tasks and then of  course we looked at approximations of  how many cells you should be able to  expect from each of these passages doing  it one way or the other and how many  vials you can freeze down to create a  bank and how many doses you can expect  to manufacture and so finally we really  wanted to make sense of all of this data  and we wanted to kind of put it into  terms that would make sense to other  laboratories that were in the same  situation as a smaller laboratories I  want to  or a clinical trial at their institution  that have limited resources and so what  we did was we imagined a hypothetical  clinical trial that happening at our  institution where each subject is  supposed to receive one dose of 100  million ms CS and we wanted to ask  whether or not it would be possible for  us to manufacture the doses needed for  this clinical trial and how much would  it cost us to do it so looking at cost  what we found was that the manufacturer  of these hundred doses using the quantum  they would cost about a hundred thousand  dollars or a thousand dollars for those  or ten dollars per million MSP however  doing it a manual way using multi layer  flasks in a single cell culture  incubator it would cost about two  hundred thousand dollars to create the  same number of doses and so the costs  were about double and the majority of  that cost came really from the cell  culture media and cell culture  supplements because doing the manual  method using multi layer flasks that  require such large volumes of media it  just adds up the cost greatly and so the  majority of that increased cost really  came from the huge amounts of media that  you needed to do this procedure manually  versus using the automated quantum  system looking at this in terms of time  if you have just a single quantum it  takes it was to take about 13 passage to  runs to create the hundred doses that  you need to run this trial if you  perform all of those runs  back-to-back non-stop they would take  approximately twenty weeks or five  months to perform all of the  manufacturing activities needed to  generate those doses although it is nice  to have two technologists to perform  these tasks if it came down to it you  really only need one to do everything  because the tasks are so automated and  that one technologist only needs to  spend about thirty four total hours of  hands-on work to do everything required  in order to generate all of these doses  in contrast using the manual method you  would need to perform five runs and  within that and each run is defined as  pulling a vial from the master cell bank  and expanding out the passage to and  then a passage three to get your your  batch if you do all those batch runs  back-to-back it takes just a little  longer about twenty four weeks or six  months the original primary culture can  be done with one technologist but all of  the works that subsequent to that you  really need at least two technologists  to perform these tasks efficiently and  with those two technologists each one of  them would need to expend almost 200  hours of hands-on time doing all of the  manual tasks required to harvest all of  those 48 flats that you could fit into  one cell culture incubator if you have  additional incubator space you can have  more growth area and therefore more cell  yields but then again you'll you're  going to expend more medium and you're  going to need even more label labor to  handle the increase in flasks and  processing time so you really need more  people to handle that many flasks is the  takeaway points if you want to not  compromise the quality of your product  and the viability of the cells so in  summary we found that using the quantum  automated system instead of using the  manual multi layer flask based protocols  resulted in almost a 50% reduction in  the cost of reagents and consumables  needed to produce 100 doses of MSDS to  run this hypothetical clinical trial and  using the automated method resulted and  about a 17 percent decrease in the  amount of actual time needed to grow out  this many cells and using the automated  method resulted in an 82 percent  reduction  the number of hours needed to be  expended by the technologists in order  to do all this work and only require the  effort of one technologist versus two or  more technologists to do all the manual  tasks efficiently and we've concluded  that we are able to produce large  quantities of bone marrow mzingo mole  stem cells for clinical use using the  automated quantum system and that it  requires significantly less cost time  and effort on our part to do this over  the manual method using certain multi  layer flasks and that indeed is B method  the platform that we have gone with for  all of our future manufacturing needs  you  excellent presentation Athena thanks for  bringing that information to us we now  welcome Brent rice PhD from the cell  processing team at Tru mo BCT who is a  collaborator with Athena and now joins  us to answer your questions before we  get started with the Q&amp;A sessions here's  a quick reminder about how to reach us  today questions can be sent David green  Q&amp;A button at the lower left of the  presentation window we'll get to as many  as we can our first question is did you  do a comparability study between sell  products grown in the flask and cells  grown in the bioreactor  yes indeed Athena did perform of  comparability study according to their  critical quality attributes so they're  indications of identity purity potency  and safety and they found that there was  no significant difference between the  two populations  I should also generally say that this is  something that is pursued by many and  probably most of our customers anybody  with a historical process of course has  their defined critical quality  attributes and then they test to ensure  that the cells produced by the quantum  system maintain these critical quality  and quality attributes H does your cost  include disposable cost of the quantum  yes the analysis that Athena provided  there are many line items one of which  is the the quantum disposables or  several of which are the quantum  disposables just as she also lists the  hyper flasks for the manual process and  one entity and handy asks could I get a  copy of the presentation and the poster  mentioned the the slides is currently  aren't available for distribution  however to my understanding this  presentation itself should be available  for the next six months as of today on  the labyrinth lab routes website okay  we have time for one last question if we  didn't get yours we will follow up the  view  email last question is have cells from  the quantum being used in patients and  fit require a GMT facility so yes you  know at this point the quantum system  has been used to produce many cells that  have been used in a variety of  treatments certainly early on more of  this use was in the context of  compassionate use or Hospital exemption  so for example treatment of GVHD as a  third line treatment or you know again a  last case resort for that that horrible  disease however thankfully you know over  the years more and more people have  submitted IMD's and more and more of  these IDs in the US are coming to  fruition in clinical studies so a couple  of examples would be for exempt or eval  disease and in glioma treatment and then  in the EU as well we're seeing a similar  pattern the most common are the most  significant clinical trial actually in  the world is in the EU where they've  treated well over 50 patients for heart  failure with